High Performance Mark makes an interesting point about floating point and associativity.  This can easily be tested (in C). float a = -1.0E8f, b = 1.0E8f, c = 1.23456f;
printf("sum %f\n", a+b+c);   //output 1.234560
printf("sum %f\n", a+(b+c)); //output 0.000000 But I would like to point out it is possible to preserve order in OpenMP.  I discussed this here C++ OpenMP: Split for loop in even chunks static and join data at the end Edit: Actually, I confused commutativity and associativity.  If you have an operator which is associative but not commuative than it's possible to preserve the order with OpenMP as I did in the post above.  However, IEEE floating point is commutative but NOT asssociative so the only thing that came be done is to break IEEE and let it be associative.

