answers.body:
For large enough matrix, mat-vec-mul is a memory bandwidth bounded operation instead of a cpu bounded operation, which means your speed is limited by reading/writing matrix data from/to RAM. In this case you won't get expected speed-up by using multi-threads. The 3rd figure of this link shows when the matrix is large enough (larger than cache), the performance drops a lot for mat-vec-mul . As the cache size and RAM bandwidth often much lower on ARM than desktop CPUs, you could get more penalties on multi-threading. On the other hand, it is usually a very bad practice to implement basic matrix/vector operations by your own when you don't know the tricks/theories inside. The good way is to use existing high performance BLAS libraries such as OpenBLAS, MKL, cuBLAS, Eigen ... Since you are using modern C++ on ARM, I would suggest you use Eigen . It has much more friendly API compared to OpenBLAS, which also has ARM-optimized code path. And you could control the multi-threading to compare the performance with Eigen very easily. All you need to do is something like: int len = 1000;
Eigen::MatrixXf mat = Eigen::MatrixXf::Random(len,len);
Eigen::VectorXf in = Eigen::VectorXf::Random(len);
Eigen::VectorXf out(len);

int num_threads = 4;
Eigen::setNbThreads(num_threads);

out = mat * in;

